"Funding a Fair Deal - Liberal Democrats (https://www.libdems.org.uk/sums)\nciteturn15view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.libdems.org.uk/sums\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 83\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2:   * 【1†Take Action】\nL3:   * 【2†Explore】\nL4: \nL5: ## 【3†For Everyone】\nL6: \nL7:   * 【4†Attend Events†events.libdems.org.uk】\nL8:   * 【5†Back our campaigns】\nL9:   * 【6†Become a volunteer】\nL10:   * 【7†Vote for us】\nL11:   * 【8†Sign up for email updates】\nL12:   * 【9†Work for us】\nL13: \nL14: ## 【10†For Members】\nL15: \nL16: 【11†Join from £15/year】\nL17: \nL18:   * 【12†Attend Conference】\nL19:   * 【13†Become an activist】\nL20:   * 【14†Become a candidate】\nL21:   * 【15†Members' Area】\nL22: \nL23:   * 【16†Donate】\nL24:   * 【17†Join】\nL25: ## 【18†About Us】\nL26: \nL27:   * 【19†Our party】\nL28:   * 【20†Our people】\nL29:   * 【21†Latest news】\nL30:   * 【22†In your community】\nL31: \nL32: ## 【23†What we stand for】\nL33: \nL34:   * 【23†For a Fair Deal - Manifesto 2024】\nL35:   * 【24†Our values】\nL36:   * 【25†Our campaigning victories】\nL37: ## 【26†Our Networks】\nL38: \nL39:   * 【27†Business Network†libdems.biz】\nL40:   * 【28†Liberty Network†www.liberty-network.org.uk】\nL41:   * 【29†The Future Fund】\nL42:   * 【30†Lib Dem City eXchange】\nL43:   * 【31†Affiliated Organisations】\nL44:   * 【32†Diversity Groups】\nL45:   * 【33†International】\nL46: \nL47:   * 【16†Donate】\nL48:   * 【17†Join】\nL49: \nL50: 【34†Image】\nL51: \nL52: # FUNDING A FAIR DEAL\nL53: ## Manifesto Costings Summary 2024\nL54: \nL55: The Liberal Democrat manifesto is a bold, ambitious and fully-costed plan to give the British people the fair deal they deserve. And we will pay for it fairly, too.\nL56: \nL57: After years of Conservative chaos and neglect, there is no doubt that our NHS, social care, schools and other public services desperately need more investment. GPs are overstretched, hospitals are overcrowded and school buildings are crumbling.\nL58: At the same time, families and pensioners are struggling in the face of a cost-of-living crisis. The Conservatives have already put up their taxes by freezing income tax thresholds. It would be wrong to ask them to pay more to clear up the Conservatives’ mess.\nL59: That’s why we will fund our fair deal without raising income tax, national insurance contributions or VAT. Instead, we will focus on tax changes that make the system fairer – such as reversing the Conservatives’ tax cuts for the big banks and imposing a proper windfall tax on the super-profits of oil and gas companies.\nL60: Responsible public finances are essential for the stability, certainty and confidence that drive economic growth, and for getting mortgage rates under control. The Conservative Government has shown all too clearly the damage and pain caused by fiscal irresponsibility. Under our plans, we will get the national debt falling as a share of the economy and ensure that day-to-day spending does not exceed the amount raised in taxes.\nL61: \nL62: 【35†Image: Funding a Fair Deal】 【36†Download File】\nL63: 【23†Liberal Democrat Manifesto】\nL64: \nL65: The Liberal Democrats will use your contact details to send you information on the topics you have requested. Any data we gather will be used in accordance with our privacy policy at 【37†www.libdems.org.uk/privacy】. To exercise your legal data rights, email: data.protection@libdems.org.uk.\nL66: \nL67: We take accessibility and your data privacy seriously. Read our 【38†accessibility statement】 and 【39†cookie policy】.\nL68: Promoted by the 【40†Liberal Democrats†libdems.org.uk】, First Floor, 66 Buckingham Gate, London SW1E 6AU.\nL69: Website by 【41†Prater Raines†praterraines.co.uk】.\nL70: \nL71: This website uses cookies\nL72: \nL73: Please select the types of cookies you want to allow.\nL74: \nL75: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL76: \nL77: [Input][Input] Essential These are necessary for the website to function properly.\nL78: \nL79: [Input][Input] Analytics These help us to understand how our visitors use our website.\nL80: [Input][Input] Marketing These allow us to display content from other websites that track you for advertising purposes.\nL81: \nL82: [Input] [Input]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPolicies | Reform UK (https://www.reformparty.uk/policies)\nciteturn15view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.reformparty.uk/policies\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 300\nL0: 【0†Image†www.facebook.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Image: Lest we forget】\nL3: \nL4: Open menu\nL5: \nL6: 【2†HOME】 【3†POLICIES】 【4†MEMBERSHIP】 【5†GET INVOLVED】 【6†SHOP NEW】 【7†DONATE】 【8†MEMBER PORTAL】 【4†Join Reform UK】\nL7: \nL8: # OUR POLICIES\nL9: \nL10: 【9†VIEW POLICIES】\nL11: ## Britain is Broken. Britain needs Reform.\nL12: \nL13: Two lost decades of economic growth. The crisis in the Channel. Lawless streets. Every problem we face comes from the same root: weak, failed Uniparty leadership.\nL14: \nL15: This ends the moment Nigel Farage enters Downing Street. Our pledge to you is simple: within 100 days of taking office, Reform UK ministers will have delivered meaningful change. If they fail, they will resign. If they refuse to resign, they will be sacked.\nL16: Within 100 days of taking power, we will put the Royal Navy in the Channel to stop the boats. We will raise the personal allowance to £15,000. We will cut £250 off your energy bills. And we will cut back the bloated university sector to fund apprenticeships.\nL17: \nL18: Britain is Broken. Britain needs Reform.\nL19: \nL20: 【10†EXPLORE OUR POLICIES】\nL21: \nL22: 【11†PDF Policy Document Economic Rescue Plan Reform UK's Plan to Save Britain's Economy View Document】\nL23: 【12†PDF Policy Document Ending the Asylum Scam Our plan to abolish Britain's broken asylum system View Document】\nL24: \nL25: 【13†PDF Policy Document The First 100 Days: Our Pledges to You Our first four pledges to the British people View Document】\nL26: \nL27: 【14†PDF Policy Document Stop The Boriswave The cost of Boris Johnson's immigration policies — a £20,000 bill for every British household. View Document】\nL28: 【15†PDF Policy Document Operation Fortress Stopping the invasion of Britain. Our plan to stop the boats and secure our borders. View Document】\nL29: \nL30: 【16†PDF Policy Document MoU: Reform UK and Rassemblement National Memorandum of Understanding between Reform UK and Rassemblement National View Document】\nL31: \nL32: 【17†PDF Policy Document Operation Restoring Justice Our plan to detain and deport all illegal migrants in the UK. View Document】\nL33: 【18†PDF Policy Document Keeping Criminals Behind Bars Our plan to end early release, make dangerous criminals serve their full sentences, and deport foreign offenders. View Document】\nL34: \nL35: 【19†PDF Policy Document Contract with Small Business Our contract with Britain's 5.7 million small businesses — fairer taxes, cheaper hiring and energy, better access to finance, and less red tape. View Document】\nL36: 【20†PDF Policy Document Affordable Social Housing for Working Britain Our plan to build 50,000 new affordable homes a year for British workers, funded with no new taxes and no government borrowing. View Document】\nL37: \nL38: 【21†PDF Policy Document Apprenticeship Wage Credit Our plan to revitalise apprenticeships with a 30% wage credit for SMEs, saving over £4,000 a year per apprentice and delivering the skilled workforce Britain needs. View Document】\nL39: 【22†PDF Policy Document Making Welfare Work Reform UK's plan for welfare. Get Britain working, put British citizens first, and protect the severely disabled. View Document】\nL40: \nL41: 【23†PDF Policy Document Agriculture & Fishing Our principles for restoring confidence, profitability, and long term sustainability to British agriculture and fishing. View Document】\nL42: \nL43: 【24†PDF Policy Document Planning Teal Paper Our plan to halt Britain’s housing crisis and reverse the decline of our town centres View Document】\nL44: 【25†PDF Policy Document Election Integrity Bill Reform UK's bill to protect the integrity of our democracy. View Document】\nL45: \nL46: Featured Policy Document\nL47: ## Economic Rescue Plan\nL48: \nL49: Reform UK's Plan to Save Britain's Economy\nL50: \nL51: 【11†Read Document】 【26†Download PDF】\nL52: \nL53: What We Stand For\nL54: \nL55: ## Our Policies\nL56: \nL57: Reform UK stands for a confident, sovereign, prosperous Britain. A country that rewards effort, enforces the law, defends its people, and believes in its future.\nL58: \nL59: 01\nL60: ### Operation Fortress: Stop the Boats\nL61: \nL62: Deploy the Royal Navy in the Channel.\nL63: \nL64: Read full policy\nL65: \nL66: Britain's borders have been left undefended for years. Over 200,000 people have landed on our shores by boat illegally over the last 8 years.\nL67: \nL68: Most of them are military aged men, including murderers and terrorists. Some have raped and murdered British people, some have attempted terror attacks.\nL69: As a complement to Operation Restoring Justice, a Reform UK Government will deploy the Armed Forces to secure our borders and stop the invasion within our First 100 Days in office.\nL70: \nL71: Under a Nigel Farage Government, not a single illegal migrant crossing the Channel will make it to our shores.\nL72: Under Reform UK's plan, titled Operation Fortress, the Royal Navy, supported by relevant civilian authorities, would intercept all migrant boats entering the English Channel, securing those on board and safely returning them to France or Belgium under escort.\nL73: \nL74: Frequently Asked Questions\nL75: \nL76: What about international law?\nL77: Our plan will also be a lawful exercise of immigration powers under maritime law, and is compliant with the duties of contracting parties under UNCLOS (United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea) and SOLAS (International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea).\nL78: \nL79: 02\nL80: ### Deport All Illegal Migrants\nL81: \nL82: A clear and uncompromising plan to remove all illegal migrants from the UK.\nL83: \nL84: Read full policy\nL85: \nL86: We have announced a clear and uncompromising plan to remove all illegal migrants from the UK. Operation Restoring Justice.\nL87: \nL88: Reform UK will implement a five-year emergency programme to identify, detain, and deport illegal migrants in the UK.\nL89: We will leave the European Convention on Human Rights, repeal the Human Rights Act and pass legislation to ensure that anyone who enters the country illegally will be ineligible for asylum.\nL90: \nL91: We will establish a UK Deportation Command and use all the available levers of the state to identify illegal migrants and deport them, including rapidly building detention centres.\nL92: The Foreign Office will secure return agreements with other countries as a matter of priority and up to five deportation flights per day will be chartered by the Home Office. Countries that refuse to take back their nationals will be subject to visa sanctions.\nL93: \nL94: We will pass a 'Deliveroo Law' to make CEOs personally and criminally liable for employing illegal migrants.\nL95: \nL96: Frequently Asked Questions\nL97: \nL98: How many people will be deported?\nL99: All illegal migrants currently in the UK will be identified and deported through Operation Restoring Justice.\nL100: \nL101: 03\nL102: ### Net Negative Immigration\nL103: \nL104: The era of cheap, low-skilled foreign labour is over.\nL105: \nL106: Read full policy\nL107: \nL108: The era of cheap, low-skilled foreign labour is over. Reform UK will end the scam of mass immigration and finally put British citizens f irst.\nL109: The Conservative Party's failed immigration policies allowed 3.8 million people (96% non-EU) into the country on long-term visas during the Boriswave (2021-2024). About half of these migrants will never work. Between 2026-2030, 1.6-2.2 million of these migrants are expected to receive Indefinite Leave to Remain (ILR), giving them access to benefits, ultimately costing the UK taxpayer around £622 billion in real terms.\nL110: Reform UK will abolish ILR and rescind existing awards. Reform UK will introduce a 5 year renewable visa for migrants with higher salary thresholds, mandatory fluency in English, and stricter good character requirements. There will be no recourse to benefits for foreign nationals.\nL111: \nL112: Frequently Asked Questions\nL113: \nL114: What is ILR and why abolish it?\nL115: \nL116: Indefinite Leave to Remain gives migrants permanent residency and access to benefits. Our position is that only British nationals should receive welfare.\nL117: What replaces ILR?\nL118: \nL119: A 5-year renewable visa with higher salary thresholds, mandatory English fluency, and stricter character requirements.\nL120: \nL121: Will there still be work visas?\nL122: \nL123: Yes, but only for critical national shortages, with caps and requirements to train British workers.\nL124: \nL125: Does this apply to those with EUSS?\nL126: No - those with EU settled status are not in scope of the policy. However, your entitlement to benefits will change under a Reform Government. Foreign nationals will no longer be eligible for welfare benefits.\nL127: \nL128: Will pensioners be affected?\nL129: \nL130: Foreign nationals of pension age who've made sufficient National Insurance Contributions to be eligible for the state pension will continue to have a residence right in the UK and receive the state pension.\nL131: \nL132: 04\nL133: ### Our NHS needs Reform\nL134: \nL135: The NHS will remain free at the point of use.\nL136: \nL137: Read full policy\nL138: \nL139: Under a Reform UK Government, the NHS will remain free at the point of use and funded by general taxation. We will improve the NHS by working to redirect funding from back office bloat back into frontline services. Successive Conservative and Labour Governments have failed our NHS, leaving patient satisfaction and clinical outcomes at record lows.\nL140: \nL141: Frequently Asked Questions\nL142: \nL143: What does free at the point of use mean?\nL144: There will be no changes to how you currently access the NHS. British citizens will not be required to pay.\nL145: \nL146: 05\nL147: ### Raise the Personal Allowance to £15,000\nL148: \nL149: Work should always be rewarded.\nL150: \nL151: Read full policy\nL152: \nL153: In the First 100 days of a Reform UK Government, we will release a Budget that increases the tax-free personal allowance to £15,000. That will take effect at the beginning of the next tax year, and it will repair much of the damage done by successive chancellors’ neglect. It will deliver £486 in tax relief to most taxpayers and take 2.9 million taxpayers out of income tax altogether.\nL154: It will be the single largest increase in the Personal Allowance in history.\nL155: \nL156: Our clear, costed programme of welfare reform, foreign aid cuts, and civil service retrenchment will deliver more than enough money to fund this and our other commitments.\nL157: \nL158: Frequently Asked Questions\nL159: \nL160: What about raising the PA to £20,000?\nL161: \nL162: As our reforms bear fruit, we will make progress towards our long-term ambition of a £20,000 personal allowance.\nL163: \nL164: How does this help families?\nL165: Lower taxes and reduced financial burden means families keep more of what they earn.\nL166: \nL167: Will benefits be reduced?\nL168: \nL169: The welfare system will support those who genuinely need help, but will no longer trap people in dependency.\nL170: \nL171: 06\nL172: ### End Lawless Britain\nL173: \nL174: The first duty of the state is to protect its citizens.\nL175: \nL176: Read full policy\nL177: \nL178: The first duty of the state is to protect its citizens.\nL179: \nL180: Reform UK will restore visible policing, much tougher sentencing for serious and repeat offenders - including mandatory life sentences for child rapists, and an uncompromising approach to crime and antisocial behaviour.\nL181: \nL182: We will deploy stop and search to get deadly weapons off our streets, and keep people safe.\nL183: We will rapidly build prison capacity and deport foreign offenders to their home country or places like El Salvador and Kosovo.\nL184: \nL185: A Reform Government would not allow early release from prison for serious crimes including murder, serious violent crimes and rape.\nL186: \nL187: Victims will come first, not criminals. The law will protect those who obey it, and punish those who break it.\nL188: \nL189: Frequently Asked Questions\nL190: \nL191: Will Reform UK increase police presence?\nL192: Yes, we will restore visible policing on our streets and deploy more officers where needed.\nL193: \nL194: What about prison overcrowding?\nL195: \nL196: We will rapidly build new prison capacity so violent criminals serve their full sentences.\nL197: \nL198: How will sentencing change?\nL199: \nL200: Mandatory minimum sentences for serious and repeat offenders, with victims prioritised over criminals.\nL201: \nL202: 07\nL203: ### Cut Energy Bills by £250 and Scrap Net Zero\nL204: \nL205: British households and industry are being crushed by the highest energy costs in the world.\nL206: \nL207: Read full policy\nL208: \nL209: British households and businesses are being crushed by among the highest energy costs in the world - driven by bad ideological policy.\nL210: \nL211: Reform UK will prioritise energy security, expand domestic energy production, and scrap policies that drive bills higher while making Britain more dependent on foreign energy.\nL212: Reform UK will remove all government imposed policy costs from energy bills. We will shift the burden of poorly thought-through renewable subsidies to the Treasury while we pursue bad actors and unwind unfair contracts.\nL213: \nL214: A Reform UK Government would take these measures within 100 days of taking office. Together, these changes would put an extra £250 a year back into your pockets.\nL215: \nL216: Frequently Asked Questions\nL217: \nL218: Will Reform UK abandon all environmental policies?\nL219: We will scrap policies that increase bills without practical benefit, while supporting sensible environmental stewardship.\nL220: \nL221: What about renewable energy?\nL222: \nL223: We support energy that works - including nuclear - but oppose subsidies that burden consumers.\nL224: \nL225: 08\nL226: ### Reverse the Jobs Tax and Reward Hard Work\nL227: \nL228: Cut taxes and put British workers first.\nL229: \nL230: Read full policy\nL231: \nL232: Labour's rise in employer National Insurance is a tax on taking a chance: on giving a young worker their first job, or expanding to another location.\nL233: \nL234: Reform is the only major political party to commit to reversing the Jobs Tax for British workers imposed by Rachel Reeves.\nL235: \nL236: We would cut the employer NIC rate from 15% back to 13.8%, funded by a new Migrant Labour Levy for employers.\nL237: We will also introduce a Hard Work Bonus, meaning employees earning under £75,000 would pay no tax on overtime worked above a 40-hour week.\nL238: \nL239: Frequently Asked Questions\nL240: \nL241: What about higher earners?\nL242: \nL243: An uncapped personal allowance for overtime above a 40-hour working week covers 90% of workers\nL244: \nL245: What rate will the Migrant Labour Levy be set at?\nL246: The levy will be tapered across pay bands, starting at National Living Wage, falling for those paying higher rate tax and then falling further for those paying the most tax.\nL247: \nL248: 09\nL249: ### Back Small Business Owners\nL250: \nL251: Entrepreneurs create jobs, growth, and opportunity.\nL252: \nL253: Read full policy\nL254: \nL255: Small and medium businesses are the lifeblood of the British economy. Together, the United Kingdom’s 5.7 million small-to-medium enterprises employ 16.9 million people, provide life to our high streets, deliver much needed services to us all, and – if everything goes well – make money for the entrepreneurs that started them.\nL256: We will make tax fairer and easier by increasing the VAT threshold to £150,000, introducing a Hard Work Bonus by removing tax on overtime, reversing Rachel Reeves’s Inheritance Tax raid on family farms and small businesses, and introducing ‘delay repay’ for HMRC backlogs, so the Revenue is incentivised to treat taxpayers with respect. We will also ease the regulatory burden on companies.\nL257: \nL258: 10\nL259: ### Rebuild Britain's Armed Forces\nL260: \nL261: Our Armed Forces have been cut to the bone.\nL262: \nL263: Read full policy\nL264: \nL265: We believe in peace through strength.\nL266: \nL267: We will rebuild Britain's armed forces, invest in capability and readiness, end woke, and restore morale across the military and intelligence services.\nL268: \nL269: We will ensure our armed forces are ready to deal with the ever-present threats posed by Russia and China.\nL270: \nL271: Britain must once again be respected by allies and feared by adversaries.\nL272: \nL273: Frequently Asked Questions\nL274: How much will defence spending increase?\nL275: \nL276: We will invest significantly in capability, readiness, and equipment for our forces. We will commit to spending 3.5% of GDP on defence by 2035.\nL277: \nL278: What does ending woke in the military mean?\nL279: \nL280: Focusing on military effectiveness and combat readiness, not political ideology.\nL281: \nL282: Learn More\nL283: ## Stay Informed\nL284: \nL285: Keep up to date with Reform UK news and find answers to common questions.\nL286: \nL287: 【27†Frequently Asked Questions Find answers to common questions about Reform UK and our policies. Read FAQs】\nL288: \nL289: 270 k+\nL290: \nL291: Members & Growing\nL292: \nL293: ## Be Part of the Party\nL294: \nL295: Join 270,000+ members who believe Britain deser